}
.status::before{ content:''; width:.5em; height:.5em; border-radius:50%; background:#7f8bb5; }
.status.saving, .status.saved, .status.error { opacity:1 }
.status.saving::before{ background:#fcd34d }
.status.saved::before{ background:#34d399 }
.status.error::before{ background:#f87171 }
```
Hmm, dot colour #34d399 on light text — the dot is decorative so contrast not required for text. Text color #c9d4ff on rgb(8 10 24 / .55) over dark navy → very high contrast (>10:1). But at opacity .55 effective contrast drops. Requirement: "all foreground text must meet 4.5:1". At opacity .55 over near-black, effective luminance... #c9d4ff at 55% alpha over #0a0a14: blend = 0.55*color + 0.45*bg → roughly (111, 118, 152) → relative luminance ~0.19 → contrast vs #0a0a14 (L~0.006) ≈ 4.3:1. Marginal. Let me raise default opacity to .7 and lighten text to #dbe3ff. Blend at .7: 0.7*219+0.3*10 = 156 for R; G: .7*227+.3*10=162; B: .7*255+.3*20=204 → (156,162,204) luminance ≈ 0.36 → contrast (0.36+0.05)/(0.006+0.05)= 7.3:1. 

Also "low-opacity by default and higher on change": .7 default, 1 on change. Hmm, "low by default": maybe .65 default → still ~6.8:1. Use .7/1.

Hmm, but the blob layer may be brighter behind the pill. With blobs at opacity .5 screen, the bottom region could be lighter, which only increases text luminance for light text? No—lighter background reduces contrast for light text. Blob colours #22d3ee etc. at .5 opacity + screen could make local bg moderately bright. Text is #dbe3ff (bright). Contrast against mid-tone cyan #22d3ee (L≈0.55)? Text luminance ~0.75 → contrast (0.8)/(0.6) = 1.3 → fails. But blobs at .5 opacity over dark, blurred: effective brightness maybe #1e3a4a-ish. The pill also has its own dark translucent background (rgb(8 10 24/.55)) which darkens behind text. To be safe, increase pill background alpha to .72 and keep text light. Let me use background rgba(6,8,20,.72). Then blended bg behind text ≈ 0.72*(8,10,24) + 0.28*(behind) — with behind being bright blob (#3ad 0.5)... approximate worst case (30,45,60) → L≈0.03 → text #dbe3ff L≈0.75 → contrast ~10:1. Good.

